<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fengxiang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fengxiang is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baoji">Baoji</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the plain north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a>. Near it lay Yong, the capital and ritual centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> from 677 BCE, whose great tombs have been excavated nearby. In the Tang period Fengxiang was a major prefecture and military governorship guarding the western approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-maozhen">Li Maozhen</a>'s Qi state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fengxiang.jpg" alt="Fengxiang" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Slb nsk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChinaBaojiFengxiang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
